## 3. the only process on this device whose cmdline mentions 'zl1-debug' is THE COMMAND ITSELF:
1219622: bash -c n=0; for p in /proc/[0-9]*; do c=$(tr "\0" " " < "$p/cmdline" 2>/dev/null); case "$c" in *zl1-debug*) n=$((n+1)); printf "%s: %s\n" "${p#/proc/}" "$c" ;; esac; done; echo "processes whose cmdline contains zl1-debug: $n" 
processes whose cmdline contains zl1-debug: 1
#   ^ the 1 is pid 1219622, i.e. the census command printed above it -- its own text contains the
#     pattern. That is the defect docs 165 fixes, reproduced on the device in four lines: the OLD reader
#     matched by SUBSTRING over the whole cmdline, and its own text was in its own argv, so it reported
#     ITSELF. The new one matches by argv POSITION (argv[0] is the path, or argv[0] is a shell and
#     argv[1] the path), so a `bash -c '<text>'` process is not a keeper no matter what its text says.
#     There is NO keeper on this boot, which is what section 1's `keeper: gone` says.

## 4. the shipped matcher run BY HAND, verbatim text extracted from the shipped script, twice:
# positive control: 128538 IS the netwatch, running as `/bin/sh /etc/systemd/system/zl1-netwatch.sh`
# (section 2), so the argv rule's second half finds a running `sh <script>` process on this device.
### KEEPER='/etc/systemd/system/zl1-netwatch.sh'
netwatch: file=present fn=has-ensure_addrs unit=active
keeper: 128538
addrs: 192.168.2.15/24=present 10.15.19.82/24=present
governors: interactive interactive interactive interactive

# negative control: the keeper's own path, on a boot where nothing runs it -- `gone`, and section 3
# shows nothing could have been found. Note the program did NOT report itself in either run.
### KEEPER='/usr/local/sbin/zl1-debug-net.sh'
netwatch: file=present fn=has-ensure_addrs unit=active
keeper: gone
addrs: 192.168.2.15/24=present 10.15.19.82/24=present
governors: interactive interactive interactive interactive

